# Build outputs and caches.
**/dist/
**/.next/
**/out/
**/coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Generated by Next.js; regenerated on every build and not ours to format. This entry is NOT made
# redundant by the .gitignore rule — Prettier does not read .gitignore, and the file is present in
# every working tree after any `pnpm dev` or `pnpm build`. It happens to satisfy Prettier today, so
# this line looks unnecessary; it is what stops a future change to Next's emitted formatting from
# turning `pnpm format:check` red for a file nobody wrote.
apps/*/next-env.d.ts

# Generated/vendored bytes that must not be reformatted.
pnpm-lock.yaml

# The OpenAPI document, vendored byte-for-byte from astro-mine-api's own committed snapshot. The
# drift gate compares the two as BYTES, so a Prettier pass here would turn a contract check into an
# argument about JSON style — and would make every upstream refresh show a diff nobody made.
#
# This applies to the document only. The TypeScript under src/generated/ IS formatted, by the
# generator itself, so `pnpm format:check` stays green without an exemption that would also hide a
# hand edit.
packages/api-client/openapi/openapi.json

# Core's units schema and its conformance vectors, vendored byte-for-byte from astro-mine-platform
# (ui#6). Exactly the reasoning above for the OpenAPI document, and discovered the same way: the
# first `pnpm format` after the port reformatted `conformance.json`, and the drift gate immediately
# reported bytes that no longer matched the platform's. A formatter and a byte-equality guard cannot
# both own a file.
#
# The TypeScript generated FROM these — src/frames/generated/units.ts — is formatted, by the
# generator itself, using this repository's own Prettier config.
packages/view/src/frames/schema/*.json
